MAY EXAMPLES
============

NAME

    counter - minimal stateful May contract

SOURCE

    examples/counter.may

DESCRIPTION

    The counter example shows the smallest useful May shape:

        model
        state
        fn
        must bounds
        assignment body

    The model declares the storage shape.

    The state names one valid contract state over that model.

    The fn declares a transition from Ready as before to Ready as after.

    The body describes the state update.

    The must block describes the facts that should hold around that update.

PROGRAM

    model Counter {
        value: int

        must [ value >= 0 ]
    }

    state Ready(Counter) {
        must [ value >= 0 ]
    }

    fn increment(amount: int) when Ready as before -> Ready as after
    must [
        amount > 0,
        after.value == before.value + amount
    ]
    {
        after.value = before.value + amount;
    }

CHECKS

    value >= 0
    amount > 0
    after.value = before.value + amount
    -----------------------------------  every call
    after.value >= 0

    The top half is what the caller and the body provide.

    The bottom half is what the target state still requires.

COMMANDS

    cargo run -- check examples/counter.may
    cargo run -- verify examples/counter.may
    cargo run -- compile examples/counter.may

FILES

    examples/build/algorand/approval.teal
    examples/build/algorand/clear.teal
    examples/build/algorand/application.json
